- What strategies would you use to prevent duplicate messages during a race between fetch and WebSocket delivery?
- How would you implement optimistic UI updates for outgoing messages?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests real-time DOM updates, patch sequencing, offline-first architecture, and Service Worker integration for a collaborative design application.

## Key Concepts
- Patch sequencing with buffering for out-of-order delivery
- WebSocket reconnection with exponential backoff
- Offline storage using IndexedDB for pending mutations
- Service Worker asset caching
- DOM-based rendering vs virtual DOM diffing approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
function applyPatch(state, patch) {
  // minimal patch applier; ensure seq consistency
  const { op, shapeId, data } = patch;
  switch (op) {
    case 'add':
      state.shapes[shapeId] = data;
      break;
    case 'update':
      Object.assign(state.shapes[shapeId], data);
      break;
    case 'delete':
      delete state.shapes[shapeId];
      break;
  }
  return state;
}
```

## Integration Points and Trade-offs

| Component | Integration Point | Trade-off |
|-----------|------------------|-----------|
| WebSocket | Delta stream subscription | Complexity vs real-time consistency |
| IndexedDB | Offline mutation queue | Storage limits vs offline capability |
| Service Worker | Asset caching | Stale assets vs offline access |
| DOM Rendering | Shape display | Simplicity vs performance at scale |

