2. What strategies would you use to prevent memory leaks in the patch buffer?
3. How do you ensure idempotency when reapplying patches after resync?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of closures, Promise coordination, and cache invalidation patterns in JavaScript without DOM dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- Closures for maintaining per-URL cache state
- Promise deduplication for concurrent in-flight requests
- Stale-while-revalidate caching strategy
- Race condition prevention and cache lifecycle management

## Code Example
```javascript
function makeFetcher() {
  const cache = new Map();
  return {
    fetchMemo: (url, ttlMs) => {
      const getEntry = () => cache.get(url) || { value: null, ts: 0, inFlight: null };
      const fetchAndUpdate = async () => {
        try {
          const response = await fetch(url);
          const data = await response.json();
          cache.set(url, { value: data, ts: Date.now(), inFlight: null });
          return data;
        } catch (error) {
          const entry = getEntry();
          entry.inFlight = null;
          throw error;
        }
      };

      return {
        get: async () => {
          const entry = getEntry();
          const now = Date.now();
          const isFresh = entry.value && (now - entry.ts) < ttlMs;

          if (isFresh) return entry.value;

          if (entry.inFlight) {
            await entry.inFlight;
            return getEntry().value;
          }

          if (entry.value) {
            entry.inFlight = fetchAndUpdate();
            return entry.value;
          }

          entry.inFlight = fetchAndUpdate();
          return await entry.inFlight;
        },
        invalidate: () => cache.delete(url)
      };
    }
  };
}
```

## Demonstration
```javascript
const fetcher = makeFetcher();

// Set up two URLs with different TTLs
const api1 = fetcher.fetchMemo('https://api.example.com/data1', 5000);
const api2 = fetcher.fetchMemo('https://api.example.com/data2', 10000);

// Concurrent callers will observe stale data immediately while fresh data loads in background
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests real-world composition of runtime modules, per-tenant isolation, and advanced hooks patterns.

## Key Concepts

- React.lazy and Suspense for on-demand modules
- useSyncExternalStore to subscribe to external per-tenant data
- Memoized selectors keyed by tenantId/moduleId
- Runtime Module Registry pattern
- Safe unmount and cleanup on tenant switches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: Module registry and host wiring
import React, { createContext, useContext, Suspense, lazy } from 'react'

export const ModuleRegistryContext = createContext({ register: () => {} })

export function ModuleHost({ tenantId }) {
  const { getModuleLoader } = useContext(ModuleRegistryContext)
  const modules = ['ModuleA', 'ModuleB']
  return (
    <div>
      {modules.map((id) => {
        const Loader = lazy(getModuleLoader(id))
        return (
          <Suspense key={id} fallback={<div>Loading {id}...</div>}>
            <Loader tenantId={tenantId} />
          </Suspense>
        )
      })}
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement versioned module contracts and feature flags per tenant?
- How would you test for memory leaks and ensure complete cleanup on tenant switches?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses mastery of complex CSS layouts that blend Grid and Flexbox, with a CSS-only responsive rail toggle and accessible animation. It mirrors real-world knowledge-base UIs at scale, demanding careful height management, predictable reflow, and robust keyboard accessibility.

## Key Concepts

- CSS Grid for multi-column responsive layouts
- Flexbox for consistent card heights
- CSS-only state toggles (checkbox hack)
- Entrance animation with stagger; prefers-reduced-motion
- focus-visible and keyboard accessibility

## Code Example

```css
.grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap:1rem; }
.card { display:flex; flex-direction:column; }
@media (max-width:1024px){ .grid{ grid-template-columns: repeat(2,1fr);} }
@media (max-width:600px){ .rail{ transform: translateY(100%); } .grid{ grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
@keyframes fadeUp { from{opacity:0; transform: translateY(6px);} to{opacity:1; transform: translateY(0);} }
.card{ animation: fadeUp .25s ease forwards; animation-delay: .0s; }
.card:nth-child(2){ animation-delay:.04s; }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify accessibility and animation performance across devices?
- How would you adapt for RTL, theming, and content density?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests mastery of advanced CSS Grid usage, responsive column math, and CSS-only state toggling without JS. It also probes ability to pack variable-height tiles efficiently and to design accessible interactions.

## Key Concepts

- CSS Grid: grid-template-columns, grid-auto-rows: minmax(...), grid-auto-flow: dense

- Responsive design with media queries and CSS variables

- Accessibility: focus-visible, reduced-motion

- CSS-only UI state toggling with data attributes

## Code Example

```css
.grid{ display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr); grid-gap: var(--gap,16px); grid-auto-rows: minmax(8rem, auto); grid-auto-flow: dense; }
.grid[data-layout="dense"]{ --gap:8px; --pad:8px; }
.card{ padding: var(--pad,12px); aspect-ratio: 16/9; }
.grid > .card:focus-visible{ outline: 2px solid #5b9bd5; transform: translateY(-2px); }
@media (max-width: 1200px){ .grid{ grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); } }
@media (max-width: 800px){ .grid{ grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){ .grid, .grid > *{ transition: none; } }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to 100+ tiles with dynamic content loading while keeping layout stable?
- How would you adapt for dark mode and ensure accessible color contrast?

